WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.589
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:07.479
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:11.630
The US military has the strongest and most
diverse navy in the world.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:16.090
The US militaries fleet of aircraft carriers
is so large, it makes the US navy the second

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.590
largest airforce in the world, second only
to the actual US Air Force.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:26.230
A single Nimitz- class aircraft carrier like
the USS Ronald Reagan, a 6.2 billion dollar

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.869
nuclear powered ship, can carry the twice
aircraft as any other foreign carrier, which

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:35.180
makes it even more shocking that it was sunk
by a single diesel powered swedish submarine

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.230
during war games in 2005.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:42.990
A single submarine that cost the same as a
F-35 at 100 million dollars managed to sneak

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:48.600
by an entire carrier task force, with anti-submarine
defences to enter the red-zone and score multiple

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.800
torpedo hits on the USS Ronald Reagan, sinking
it virtually, before shrinking back into the

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.830
vast ocean undetected.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.760
This was just one of many exercises where
the Swedish Gotland class submarine proved

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.190
too stealthy for the world’s strongest navy.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.950
The new submarine proved so threatening that
the US military leased the Swedish sub for

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.759
an additional year to develop strategies to
counter the silent threat.

00:01:09.759 --> 00:01:12.240
So what set the Gotland apart from other subs.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.020
Submarines’ primary instrument to detect
enemy subs is sonar.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.000
Sonar is essentially a finely tuned ear that
works like a whales or dolphins echolocation

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.149
to create a 3D map of ocean around it.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:26.320
There is active sonar where the submarine
will send out a sound pulse called a “ping”

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.159
and listen for the reflections, but in warfare
this isn’t a sound strategy, as that ping

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:36.009
is detectable by enemies to give your exact
location, so passive sonar is used where no

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:38.909
ping is emitted and instead you simply listen.

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:43.119
These electronic ears are so accurate that
the nationality of submarines can be determined

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:48.119
based on the operating frequency of the alternating
current used in it’s power systems.

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:52.450
The 60 Hz alternating current of a US sub
could be differentiated from the 50 hz of

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.959
European subs, if the transformers and other
electronics were not adequately insulated

00:01:56.959 --> 00:01:57.959
from the hull.

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:02.179
The Swedes managed to create a submarine so
silent that it was practically undetectable

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:06.060
by passive sonar, so how did they do this
at such a low cost?

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.960
The Gotland was the first submarine in the
world to use a stirling engine as it’s power

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:11.960
generator.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.370
Stirling engines are not a new concept with
the first being created and patented by Robert

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.270
Stirling in 1816.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.291
Inspired by a series of high pressure steam
boiler explosions at the beginning of the

00:02:21.291 --> 00:02:25.880
industrial revolution, Stirling wanted to
create a safer engine that did not require

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.880
such high pressures.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.500
I can’t be arsed trying to animate this
old drawing, let’s go for something simpler.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.890
He did this by creating a closed cylinder
containing a fixed mass of gas permanently

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.330
sealed within.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.530
Here one side of the piston cylinder has a
large buffer space, which allows for a relatively

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.690
constant pressure on this side of the piston,
while the other side fluctuates in pressure

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.510
due to alternating heating cycles.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.080
When heat is applied to the outside of the
cylinder, the pressure increases causing the

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.350
piston to move until the pressure equalizes.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:58.450
Now, if we cool the outside of the cylinder
with a heat exchanger, the pressure will drop

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.840
and once again the piston will move.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.890
This is our basic pressure cycle to create
mechanical work.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.340
But, this is an insanely inefficient system,
as most of the energy we put into the system

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.840
as heat is lost during the cooling cycle,
not to the gas, but to the actual cylinder

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.360
wall, which provides no mechanical work.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.650
Robert Stirling solved this by adding a displacer
piston, which can drive the gas from one end

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.540
of the cylinder to the other.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.170
Allowing this end to be permanently hot, and
the other permanently cold.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.580
So the cylinder wall is no longer experiencing
a temperature cycle.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.270
The pressure cycle here works slightly differently.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.020
First the air in the hot end expands and causes
the displacer to move into contact with the

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.531
power piston, displacing more air from the
cold end to be heated and expanded, allowing

00:03:39.531 --> 00:03:41.580
work to be done on the power piston.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.060
The air on the hot end now has nowhere to
go, and so is driven to the cold end, where

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.240
it is cooled and contracts causing work to
be done once again on power piston.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.790
This is our new pressure cycle.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.710
The efficiency of this system can be increased
further by placing what is essentially a heat

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.910
battery in the tubes between the hot and cold
cylinder.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.390
This conserves a huge amount of heat that
would otherwise be wasted during the cooling

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.140
cycle, and gives the heat back to the air
as it travels back to through.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.510
Robert Stirling dubbed this the regenerator.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.080
Now we have the foundations of a useful engine.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.780
By incorporating a coolant system and a heating
chamber we create a larger temperature differential

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.840
to drive the engine, and the efficiency can
be future increased by increasing the number

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.430
of tubes connecting the hot and cold spaces,
along with the number of regenerators, and

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:28.020
adding fins to increase the surface area of
these tubes to allow for heat transfer.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.150
Did I say simplified?

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.599
Sorry I meant easier to read.

00:04:31.599 --> 00:04:33.750
Maybe I like the misery”

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.689
Stirling engines ultimately fell into obscurity
as stronger steel became available to make

00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:42.169
steam engine boilers safer, but have seen
a resurgence in recent decades with the Gotland

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:43.990
being the most famous implementation.

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.949
The Gotland uses two Stirling engines that
use diesel and liquid oxygen to provide heat,

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:52.090
which in turn runs it’s 75kw generators.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.050
These generators can run an electric motor
directly, or charge batteries that can provide

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.509
a huge boost in speed when needed.

00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:02.580
All the while the exhaust is compressed and
stored on board, allowing the sub to stay

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.669
submerged for up to 2 weeks vastly longer
than any other diesel power submarine.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:12.330
So why is it so silent compared to other submarines,
it doesn’t require much explanation as to

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:16.810
why using an internal combustion engine using
tiny controlled explosions for power tends

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.430
to cause some noise.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.449
While the the multi-billion dollar nuclear
powered submarines need to pump huge volumes

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:26.719
of coolant to their reactors to prevent a
meltdown, causing enough noise to be detectable

00:05:26.719 --> 00:05:28.910
by passive sonar within a certain range.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:34.129
On top of this, recently declassified intelligence
suggest that Russian subs are using these

00:05:34.129 --> 00:05:38.580
instruments to detect the faint trail of radiation
left in the wake of these nuclear powered

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.580
submarines.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.460
Giving the swedish submarine another way of
avoiding detection.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:46.180
This is a fascinating application of the laws
of thermodynamics.

